**Cripps (Mar 1942) offered Dominion status, not independence — which is why Gandhi called it "a post-dated cheque".**

The Cripps Draft Declaration promised, *after* the war:
- **Dominion status** for an Indian Union, free to leave the Commonwealth;
- a **constituent assembly** elected by provincial legislatures plus princely-state nominees;
- the right of any province **not to accede** (the seed of partition, but partition itself was not proposed);
- meanwhile, defence stayed with the British.

Congress rejected it (no immediate transfer of power, no responsibility for defence); the League rejected it for not conceding Pakistan outright.

**Article 249 — Rajya Sabha, two-thirds of members *present and voting*.**

The Council of States (never the Lok Sabha) must resolve that it is "necessary or expedient in the national interest" that Parliament legislate on a named State List entry. The resolution:
- needs a **special majority: not less than 2/3 of members present and voting** (not 2/3 of total membership);
- is **valid for one year**, renewable any number of times, one year at a time;
- the law made under it lapses **six months** after the resolution ceases.

Remember the pairing: **Art. 249 (national interest) and Art. 312 (All-India Services) both go through the Rajya Sabha with this same majority.**
